# BACKFILL_REPORT.md — 0013 legacy data preservation

## What is preserved
Every legacy grooming booking that carried a `pet_id` (and optionally a
`service_id` + snapshots) is migrated into the new model BEFORE the columns are
dropped, in `0013` SECTION 300:

- **grooming_jobs** — ensured present for each grooming booking with a pet.
- **grooming_job_pets** — one row per legacy `bookings.pet_id`; `sequence=1`;
  `status='complete'` if the booking was already completed, else `'pending'`;
  `is_required=true`.
- **grooming_job_pet_services** — one row per legacy `bookings.service_id`
  (when present), carrying `service_name_snapshot` (falling back to the catalog
  name, then `'Legacy service'`), `unit_price_snapshot = price_snapshot`,
  `currency`, `quantity=1`.

The backfill is **idempotent** (each insert guarded by a `not exists`), so
re-running the migration on a partially-migrated DB is safe.

## Validation query (SECTION 310) — pass 3
Three counts are computed:
1. grooming bookings with a `pet_id` that have **no** matching
   `grooming_job_pet`.
2. those bookings (with a `service_id`) that have **no** matching
   `grooming_job_pet_service`.
3. **(pass 3, REVIEW ITEM 13)** bookings whose `booking_type <> 'grooming'`
   that still carry a non-null `pet_id` or `service_id`. SECTION 300 backfills
   only grooming rows, so these have no mapping path and SECTION 320 would drop
   their columns and lose the data.

If **any** of the three is non-zero the migration **RAISES `BACKFILL ABORT`**
(errcode `data_exception`). Because pass-3 wraps the whole file in one
`begin; … commit;`, that raise aborts the **entire** transaction: the column
drops never run, and none of SECTIONS 000–300's work (dropped legacy effect
functions, new tables, backfill rows, new constraints) is committed either.
The `0013_backfill_harness/run_backfill_negative.sh` harness proves this on a
fresh connection after a deliberate abort.

## Rows that cannot be mapped deterministically
The backfill maps a row deterministically when: the booking is
`booking_type='grooming'`, `pet_id` is not null, and (for the service line)
`service_id` is not null. Cases that will be reported as unmapped and abort the
migration until resolved:

- A grooming booking whose `pet_id` references a pet that violates the
  composite FK (e.g. wrong org / removed) — the `grooming_job_pets` insert
  fails the FK; resolve the data first.
- A booking whose `booking_type` is not `'grooming'` but nonetheless carries a
  `pet_id`/`service_id` — **not** migrated by design (only grooming lines are
  modeled in this batch). In **pass 3** the SECTION 310 validation counts these
  explicitly (count #3 above) and **automatically aborts** the whole migration
  if any exist, so the drop can never silently discard them. To proceed, either
  clear those columns on the non-grooming rows or ship that vertical's own
  backfill (mirroring SECTION 300) first.

## Non-grooming bookings
`bookings.pet_id`/`service_id` were nullable and, in practice, used only by the
grooming path. If your dataset has other `booking_type` rows with these set,
pass-3 SECTION 310 will **refuse to apply** (BACKFILL ABORT) rather than drop
them destructively — export/clear or add a per-vertical backfill first. This is
verified by the negative harness, which seeds exactly such a poison row and
confirms the migration rolls back completely.
